A||||16||v8vinnie||high speed handling, vibration||||9||
Z||000000||v8vinnie||05-21-2001||08:33 PM||||hi guys, this weekendend i went on a long drive about 496miles, any way i started to open up my v8 and it was pulling like a mule better than my old 1997  C5 corvette, at 125mph the left headlight cover came up, so i let off,i got to fix that problem, any home made fixes?. next problem, when i did leave my headlights up some guy in a crysler 300M was playing with me , so i toasted him and stayed on it to about 135mph , but the car was getting to floaty for me, so i let off, also my front end was vibrating and shaking at 80mph pluss, i just had a 2hour alingnment done and its spot on, also running 205 45 17 in front and 235 45 17 in back, lowered with pro springs, fiero store sway bars,and poly out back and on the cradel ,kyb shocks, could a bad steering shock be doing this if its bad, i really never took it off to check it, , also one other thing some times it wanders a bit at high speeds, would a different set of shocks stop that floating feelling, also what psi are you guys running in your 17 inch tires, 44psi and 50 psi are to rough for me, what do you high speed guys think?, is it wrong alignment specks or a bad steering stabalizer shock ? thanks for any info!||||reg||9
Z||000001||KissMySSFiero||05-21-2001||09:40 PM||||at those sppeeds it very possible the tires are out of balance.  most conventional machines spiin the tire to about 70mph.  and they usually say that cant get any vibration out past that because the cant "test" the car.  at 80 and up you can feel a 1/4 oz out of balance.  hope this helps.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>      <P>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">          <BR>"When in Doubt..Whip it out!!"||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||05-21-2001||10:03 PM||||I know at some shops they have a special wheel balancer that applies a load on a tire rather than spinning freely in the air, This is a MUCH more accurate balance.  I think that if you had any other problem than balance in wheels or axle shafts it would have show up at lower speeds.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Brucepts||05-21-2001||10:33 PM||||Not that this will cure your problem but . . . When I had my four wheel alignment done after installing lowered springs and poly bushings, the guy at the alignment shop retorqued all my lug nuts to 100lbs. This was done before any alignment was performed he told me that he has seen wheel alignment pulled out by not having the same torque on all the lugs on the wheels. Don't know how true this is but I retorque my lugs to 100lbs everytime I pull a wheel and never had a problem. Course this is on a 2.5 SE which has never seen the other side of 85mph not a fire breathing V8. Still fun to drive on the ole backroads  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Archie||05-21-2001||10:46 PM||||It could be tire balance etc. or it could be too much air under the car.<P>Next time you have it up to those kinds of speed, pull the hood release and see if it settles down.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000005||KissMySSFiero||05-22-2001||06:55 AM||||The hood release also works if your cars gettin too hot.  guess i need some vents.  <BR>another note.  you obviously have aftermarket wheels.  if they dont have a lip to hammer weights to the outside some shops will do a static balance.  thats only putting weight on one side of the rim.  IMO that just flat out sucks. when you get your tires balanced request for them to use stick on weights.  you might have to clean your rims really well because techs can sometimes be too lazy to do it and theyll just fall off making them out of balance again.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||thomas_l||05-22-2001||09:17 AM||||Could be the tires too. I have had problems in the past with Pirelli and Dunlop tires requiring frequent balancing. My 87 GT does the same over 70 mph right after a 4 wheel align and two new Sumitomos on the rear (stock GT rims). The fronts are the old Pirellis that were on it when I bought it and, being the cheap bastard I am, I opted to leave them on and just replace the two with cords showing on the rear. Everything on the suspension is stock and reasonably tight but the car also has 166k on it. Sometimes the tire guys can index the tires to the rims and reduce the amount of weights that get installed. I'd go for a better wheel balance then start looking for other things that might be a little out of whack.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ThatRickGuy||05-22-2001||09:40 AM||||Headlight problem:<BR> There's a few people here who have rear facing scoops on their hoods, not cowls, but vent like deals, the look cool, promote better radiator cooling, and reduce lift on the front of the car. or you can get a quick fix, I think from Chris West, it's a little metal wire that attaches from your light cover down to the light mechinism, cheap and fast.<P>Vibrations:<BR> At those speeds, standard spin balancing isn't going to be perfect, goto a shop that has the load testing. the tire may be perfectly balanced, but a 'hard spot' in the rubber could be makeing it bounce. and like it was said early, avoice static balancing. better looking, but poor performance.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Taijiguy||05-22-2001||12:32 PM||||Might also check for a rim out of round, or with a warp in it. At high speeds, even a small amount of runout will feel like a five pound hammer on the wheel.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||05-22-2001||01:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by v8vinnie:<BR><B>hi guys, this weekendend i went on a long drive about 496miles, any way i started to open up my v8 and it was pulling like a mule better than my old 1997  C5 corvette, at 125mph the left headlight cover came up, so i let off,i got to fix that problem, any home made fixes?. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hood louvres will fix your headlight problem.<P>For the vibration: 1) Balance--allow the tech to put weights on the outside of the wheel; it may not look as good, but it is much more effective. You may have to use some specialized equipment as mentioned above.<P>2) Did you replace your wheel bearings when you did all this other work? Are they tight? worn wheel bearings might be the culprit.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||05-22-2001||04:06 PM||||I get my tires spun balanced on the car, hard to find anymore. It gets a little floaty in the front over 120-130. above that the downforce on the nose (Ferrari rebody) pushes down enough to counteract it. Gets steadier the faster I go. I eliminated the headlite prob with flush headlites. Getting sorta curious if the Ferrari roof spoiler will affect the high speed handling or not.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Taijiguy||05-22-2001||05:00 PM||||Roger...did you have that done around here? Where? That's what I want done with mine, and you're right, that's a long lost process, but about the best I think...only downside is you need to put the wheels back on the same side and orientation since it takes rotor and everything into account.||||reg||1
Z||000012||v8vinnie||05-22-2001||07:35 PM||||thanks for the quick repls, im going to have the front tires rebalanced again, also im using stick on weights, im also going to check and see if that steering shock is worn out and another thing ,i didnt realize the air lifting the front end could cause the problem , the guy i bought this car from put the battery in the front and had drilled 2 holes in the spare tie compartment just in front of the battery but did not like the extra dirt in the compartment so he put 2 plugs in the holes, you think if i remove these plugs it will let some pressure out,the wheel bearings are okay, this vibration is only in the front end ,back end is fine and i also put those azle adapters on the shafts for extra saftey, thanks again for the feed back ill let you know the results when i have them rebalanced and so forth, buy keep any suggestions you have coming in, i always brag about you guys to my motor head friends how you guys support the fieros, thats why i have one!||||reg||1
Z||000013||rogergarrison||05-22-2001||07:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Taijiguy:<BR><B>Roger...did you have that done around here? Where? That's what I want done with mine, and you're right, that's a long lost process, but about the best I think...only downside is you need to put the wheels back on the same side and orientation since it takes rotor and everything into account.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>E.T. Paul Co, on S. Parsons Ave between Broad and Main is the only one I know of. I mark one lug on left with yellow paint and a matching dab on the wheel where you cant see it. use red on other side. That way I alway get them in same position when I take them off. They can even true your tires if need be.<P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||bcwawright||05-22-2001||08:20 PM||||v8vinnie<BR>What are these azel adapters you installed on the shafts?<P>Bruce<BR>88GT||||reg||5
Z||000015||lowCG||05-22-2001||09:56 PM||||It's too bad that GM designed our cars to get the air for cooling the radiator from under the nose,not from out front.This creates some aero problems as the speed increases.<BR>I'd invest in some shocks,I use Bilsteins from other vehicles(Golf/Jetta in the rear,and Toyota pickup in front),adapted to my car.The Konis they sell were originally for something else also,and guess they're good too.||||reg||1
Z||000016||v8vinnie||05-22-2001||10:00 PM||||sorry, thats axle adapters that the fiero store sells, they go on the end of the transmission box where the shafts go in, they give the shafts a little more support.||||reg||1
